   161	
   162	### 4.1 Missed divisors and the cofinite image
   163	
   164	Let `V=g1(A2)`, an open subset of `U`.  The free group on the prime divisors
   165	contained in `U minus V` injects into `Pic(U)`.  Indeed, if a rational
   166	function `b` has divisor supported there, then `b` and `b^-1` are regular on
   167	the normal open `V`.  Their pullbacks are mutually inverse polynomials on
   168	`A2`, hence scalars.  Dominance makes `b` the same scalar, so its divisor was
   169	zero.  This proves linear independence of all missed prime divisors.
   170	
   171	The finite group (4.1) contains no nonzero free subgroup.  Thus `U minus V`
   172	has no curve component.  It is a finite set:
   173	
   174	```text
   175	g1:A2->U is an affine pseudo-covering.                         (4.2)
   176	```
   177	
   178	### 4.2 The multiple-fibre order divides the first-leg degree
   179	
   180	Choose the ruling coordinate `t` so that `div_U(t)=mF`, and put
   181	`L=C(x,y)`, `K=C(U)`.  Etaleness makes `g1^*F` a reduced divisor.  Since
   182	`C[x,y]` is factorial,
   183	
   184	```text
   185	g1^*t=c P^m,       c in C*, P in C[x,y] nonconstant.          (4.3)
   186	```
   187	
   188	The binomial `X^m-t/c` is irreducible over `K`.  For if `t/c=s^p` in `K`
   189	for a prime `p|m`, normality first gives `s in O(U)` and then
   190	
   191	```text
   192	div_U(s)=(m/p)F,
   193	```
   194	
   195	contradicting the exact order `m` of `[F]` in (4.1).  Since `C` contains all
   196	roots of unity, the standard Kummer binomial criterion has no further
   197	exception.  Equation (4.3) therefore embeds a degree-`m` field
   198	
   199	```text
   200	K(P) subset L.
   201	```
   202	
   203	The tower law gives
   204	
   205	```text
   206	m divides [L:K]=d1.                                        (4.4)
   207	```
   208	
   209	Finally, etaleness of the second leg gives the exact canonical condition
   210	
   211	```text
   212	K_U=pi^*K_A2~0.                                             (4.5)
   213	```
   214	
   215	### 4.3 Typed theorem: useful but not a coverage theorem
   216	
   217	For a pseudo-plane already known to have Miyanishi's special boundary type
   218	`(d,n,r)`, Theorem 2.5.10 computes `K_U~(r-2)F` and excludes an etale map to
   219	`A2` unless the exceptional `r=2` row occurs.  Nothing charged here proves
   220	that an arbitrary `U` satisfying (4.1)--(4.5) belongs to that typed class.
